    </div>

    <div id="allAnswers" class="d-none">
        <div id="answer1">
            <h4>Answer 1 - Generic Swap Method</h4>
            <pre><code>public static void Swap&lt;T&gt;(ref T left, ref T right) // Declares a generic method with type parameter T.
{
    T temp = left; // Stores left value temporarily to avoid data loss.
    left = right; // Assigns right value to left variable.
    right = temp; // Assigns stored original left value to right variable.
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> This method is reusable for any type (int, string, custom classes). The <code>ref</code> keyword passes variables by reference so the original values are swapped outside the method too.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer2">
            <h4>Answer 2 - Generic Pair Class</h4>
            <pre><code>public class Pair&lt;TFirst, TSecond&gt; // Defines a generic class with two independent type parameters.
{
    public TFirst First { get; set; } // Stores the first value in a strongly typed property.
    public TSecond Second { get; set; } // Stores the second value in a strongly typed property.

    public void Print() // Prints both values for verification.
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"First: {First}, Second: {Second}"); // Displays values using string interpolation.
    }
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> This class avoids duplicate classes for different combinations like int-string or string-double by using generics.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer3">
            <h4>Answer 3 - Generic Constraint with class</h4>
            <pre><code>public class Repository&lt;T&gt; where T : class // Restricts T to reference types only.
{
    private readonly List&lt;T&gt; _items = new(); // Creates storage for the generic type.

    public void Add(T item) // Adds item safely because T is guaranteed to be a reference type.
    {
        _items.Add(item); // Appends item to list.
    }
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Constraints improve safety and clarity by controlling what types can be used in generic classes.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer4">
            <h4>Answer 4 - Arithmetic Delegate</h4>
            <pre><code>public delegate int MathOperation(int a, int b); // Defines delegate signature for math methods.

public static int Add(int x, int y) => x + y; // Implements addition.
public static int Multiply(int x, int y) => x * y; // Implements multiplication.

MathOperation operation = Add; // Assigns Add method to delegate instance.
Console.WriteLine(operation(4, 5)); // Invokes delegate and prints 9.
operation = Multiply; // Reassigns delegate to Multiply method.
Console.WriteLine(operation(4, 5)); // Invokes delegate and prints 20.</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Delegates allow dynamic method assignment while keeping method signatures strongly typed.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer5">
            <h4>Answer 5 - Multicast Delegate</h4>
            <pre><code>public delegate void Logger(string message); // Creates delegate for log methods.

public static void LogToConsole(string message) // First target method.
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Console: {message}"); // Writes message to console.
}

public static void LogToFile(string message) // Second target method.
{
    Console.WriteLine($"File: {message}"); // Simulates file logging.
}

Logger logger = LogToConsole; // Adds first method to invocation list.
logger += LogToFile; // Adds second method using multicast behavior.
logger("Order processed"); // Calls both methods in sequence.</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Multicast delegates are useful for notifications where one event should trigger multiple handlers.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer6">
            <h4>Answer 6 - Func and Action with Generics</h4>
            <pre><code>Func&lt;int, int, int&gt; sum = (a, b) => a + b; // Func returns value and accepts two int inputs.
Action&lt;string&gt; print = text => Console.WriteLine(text); // Action performs operation without return.

int result = sum(10, 20); // Executes generic delegate and gets calculated result.
print($"Total: {result}"); // Prints result using Action delegate.</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Built-in generic delegates reduce custom delegate creation and improve readability.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer7">
            <h4>Answer 7 - Generic Filter with Predicate</h4>
            <pre><code>public static List&lt;T&gt; FilterList&lt;T&gt;(List&lt;T&gt; items, Predicate&lt;T&gt; condition) // Accepts generic list and condition.
{
    return items.FindAll(condition); // Returns only matching items based on predicate.
}

List&lt;int&gt; numbers = new() { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 }; // Sample integer list.
List&lt;int&gt; evenNumbers = FilterList(numbers, n => n % 2 == 0); // Filters only even values.</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Predicate-based generic filtering is reusable and keeps business logic clean.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer8">
            <h4>Answer 8 - Generic Event Dispatcher (High)</h4>
            <pre><code>public class EventDispatcher&lt;TEvent&gt; // Creates a type-safe generic dispatcher for any payload type.
{
    private event Action&lt;TEvent&gt;? OnEventRaised; // Stores subscribed handlers for the generic event.

    public void Subscribe(Action&lt;TEvent&gt; handler) // Registers a new handler.
    {
        OnEventRaised += handler; // Adds handler to invocation list.
    }

    public void Publish(TEvent data) // Raises event with strongly typed data.
    {
        OnEventRaised?.Invoke(data); // Invokes all subscribers if any are attached.
    }
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> This pattern is useful in modular applications where events carry different payload types safely.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer9">
            <h4>Answer 9 - Variance with Generic Delegates (High)</h4>
            <pre><code>public class Animal { } // Base class type.
public class Dog : Animal { } // Derived class type.

public delegate Animal AnimalFactory(); // Delegate returning base type.
public delegate void AnimalHandler(Dog dog); // Delegate accepting derived type.

Animal CreateDogAsAnimal() => new Dog(); // Returns Dog as Animal (covariance use case).
void HandleAnimal(Animal animal) { } // Accepts broader type for contravariance.

AnimalFactory factory = CreateDogAsAnimal; // Covariance: method returns derived where base expected.
AnimalHandler handler = HandleAnimal; // Contravariance: method accepts base where derived expected.</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Variance improves flexibility in delegate assignments while maintaining type safety.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="answer10">
            <h4>Answer 10 - Generic Retry Executor (High)</h4>
            <pre><code>public static T ExecuteWithRetry&lt;T&gt;(Func&lt;T&gt; operation, int retryCount) // Accepts generic operation and retry limit.
{
    for (int attempt = 1; attempt &lt;= retryCount; attempt++) // Runs loop until retry limit is reached.
    {
        try
        {
            return operation(); // Executes operation and returns immediately on success.
        }
        catch
        {
            if (attempt == retryCount) // Checks whether this is the final attempt.
            {
                throw; // Rethrows exception after all retries fail.
            }
        }
    }

    throw new InvalidOperationException("Unexpected execution path."); // Safety fallback.
}</code></pre>
            <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> This reusable pattern centralizes retry logic for API calls, database access, or unstable operations.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

